# CI and local verification

The private repository's included Actions quota was exhausted in October 2026; the
reported reset is November 1. Budget-blocked hosted checks are not code-test failures.
Do not rerun them repeatedly, change spending limits, upgrade the account, or select
billable larger runners. This integration uses local validation and does not change
billing settings. Keep the owner's paid-usage allowance at zero; a standard hosted
runner can still incur charges if an owner later authorizes paid minutes.

## Ordinary PRs and main pushes

One standard `ubuntu-latest` job runs theorem-state and promotion-transaction checks,
active-route and entrypoint checks, tool/catalogue/coordination/export unit tests,
publication dependency planning, navigation, and whitespace checks. Lightweight
PyYAML/jsonschema dependencies suffice until certificates are selected. Concurrent
superseded runs are cancelled. Tokens have only `contents: read`; checkout does not
persist credentials. PR event values enter through JSON/environment, not shell code.

Only explicitly listed workflow prose can skip certificates. Every research-code/data
change runs all registered certificates, including unregistered/transitive/dynamic
helpers and file inputs. Authority, manifest, canonical proof sources, workflows,
verification tools and unknown paths also run the full suite. This conservative
fallback avoids an unvalidated Python dependency graph. The tests exercise both skip
and must-run cases. It prioritizes soundness over finer computational selection.

PRs use the unique merge base of event base/head SHAs and check out the actual head.
Main pushes compare the entire before/after trees after confirming ancestry, event
fast-forward metadata, clean checkout, and complete history. Merge commits retain both
parents' changes. Renames are additions plus deletions; filenames use NUL delimiters.
Force pushes, missing/zero endpoints, ambiguous ancestry, shallow clones, dirty trees,
and failed diffs require full certificates. Missing transaction ancestry also makes
the integrity validator fail; a certificate pass never replaces promotion review.

## Reproduce locally

Use CPython 3.14.7 in a clean isolated worktree; keep the virtual environment outside it.
Exact transitive package versions are in `../../tools/requirements-verification.lock`.
That file pins versions across platforms; the separately measured
`tools/requirements-verification-linux-x86_64.lock` pins ten downloaded wheel hashes for
CPython 3.14.7 on Linux x86_64. `tools/verification-wheels.json` records exact filenames,
sizes and hashes. Its clean offline installation and `pip check` were exercised on
glibc 2.44. This does not pin the OS, pip, hardware or package-index trust, and no
other platform has been replayed. Unsupported wheel/platform combinations fail
installation; do not remove `--require-hashes` to suppress that failure.

```sh
python3 -m venv /tmp/e99-verification-venv
/tmp/e99-verification-venv/bin/python -m pip install --require-hashes --only-binary=:all: -r tools/requirements-verification-linux-x86_64.lock
/tmp/e99-verification-venv/bin/python tools/verify_prelaunch.py --base-ref origin/main
/tmp/e99-verification-venv/bin/python tools/verify_prelaunch.py --full --base-ref origin/main
python3 tools/run_theorem_certificates.py --base-ref origin/main --plan
```

`--full` runs all research unit tests and every registered certificate. No CI optimization
changes that command. Run the baseline utility with `--expected-commit <full SHA>` for
an exact clean-tree identity check. The historical baseline document remains pinned to
its original commit; it is not a hash promise about today's integration.

## Optional comprehensive and historical workflows

The ordinary workflow also has `workflow_dispatch`, guarded by the repository owner
and `E99_COMPREHENSIVE_CI=true`; unknown dispatch ranges select the full suite. This
variable defaults to unset and was not enabled by integration. Dispatch only after
checking the available included quota and retaining a zero paid-usage allowance.

All 21 historical Target C workflows are manual only, guarded before matrix expansion
by the owner and `E99_HISTORICAL_CI=true` (unset by default). They preserve their original
research commands and certificates but no longer run on main pushes. They can use
large shard counts and long timeouts: prefer their local commands for historical
experiments. No historical workflow or comprehensive dispatch was launched here.

## Owner configuration before public collaboration

The October 15 read-only inspection still found no protection on `main`. The latest
inspected hosted job had **zero steps** and an annotation citing failed payments or
the spending limit; that annotation does not distinguish quota from payment failure.
The November 1 reset above remains a historical report. No billing change or retry
was made to investigate it.

For a future approved destination, require PRs, at least one accountable maintainer
approval, resolved review conversations and a successful `state-consistency` check;
dismiss stale approvals after material changes. Decide who may bypass those controls
and restrict that group explicitly. Keep the two independent mathematical reviews in
the promotion policy even if branch settings cannot encode their scope. Review and
pin action revisions, and choose an update process for actions and verifier packages.
Do not require a check that cannot execute while the account is blocked, or mistake
a local test result for a hosted approval. These instructions change no live setting.

For a clean-history export rehearsal, initialize only a disposable local copy and run
the full suite with `--base-ref HEAD`. There is no `origin/main` or private historical
Git object database in the original file export. The restoration-body check is
self-contained; `check_restored_evidence.py --source-root <private-archive>` is the
separate optional source-object audit. Full prelaunch verification now has **14 stages**.

## Offline wheel rehearsal

Use a new external venv and wheel directory. Downloading with the hash lock verifies
each artifact before an offline installation; pip also verifies it again on install.

```sh
python3 -m pip download --require-hashes --only-binary=:all: -r tools/requirements-verification-linux-x86_64.lock --dest /tmp/e99-wheels
/tmp/e99-verification-venv/bin/python -m pip install --no-index --find-links /tmp/e99-wheels --require-hashes --only-binary=:all: -r tools/requirements-verification-linux-x86_64.lock
/tmp/e99-verification-venv/bin/python -m pip check
```

A one-byte wheel mutation was rejected by pip's hash check during the October 15
rehearsal. The lock-consistency test detects changes that diverge from the measured
manifest or the cross-platform version lock; it does not download packages during CI.
Keep optional privacy scanners outside the verifier runtime. Updating packages requires
a fresh measured wheel set and complete replay, with additional platforms recorded
separately. No container/platform reproducibility is claimed from version pins alone.

## Workflow wheel integrity

Both ordinary workflow install stages enforce `--require-hashes --only-binary=:all:`.
The lightweight stage uses `tools/requirements-integrity-linux-x86_64.lock`, a six-wheel
subset of the measured ten-wheel manifest; it installs no NumPy, SciPy, SymPy or mpmath.
The full measured lock remains conditional on the conservative certificate planner.
Tests bind both lock sets to the measured hashes and preserve that conditional, read-only
permissions and checkout credential settings. A fresh lightweight environment passes the
12-stage integrity suite locally. Hosted execution remains blocked before steps; no other
runner platform or operating-system image is claimed verified by that local replay.
